==============================================================================
PART A -- the polar bridge, verified exactly against Wick (random complex P)
==============================================================================
  P={(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}  ->  bridge==Wick for m=1..6: True
  P={(2, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(-3, 1), (0, 2): Fraction(2, 1)}  ->  bridge==Wick for m=1..6: True
  P={(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}  ->  bridge==Wick for m=1..6: True
  P={(2, 1): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(-2, 1), (1, 2): Fraction(3, 1)}  ->  bridge==Wick for m=1..6: True
  polar bridge confirmed: True

==============================================================================
PART B -- the charge support of Lambda_s is s-INDEPENDENT (the uniform DvdK hook)
==============================================================================
  Z^a W^b -> s^{(a+b)/2} u^{a-b}: coefficient s^{...} > 0 for s>0 never kills a charge,
  so the set of charges present in Lambda_s is constant off a finite set of s.
                                 P     charges of P  two-sided?
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}          [-1, 1]        True
{(2, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(-3, 1), (0, 2): Fraction(2, 1)}       [-2, 0, 2]        True
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}       [-1, 0, 1]        True
{(2, 1): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(-2, 1), (1, 2): Fraction(3, 1)}          [-1, 1]        True
{(3, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}          [-1, 3]        True
{(2, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 0): Fraction(1, 1)}           [1, 2]       False
  P two-sided  <=>  Lambda_s two-sided for a.e. s  =>  DvdK applies UNIFORMLY in s.

==============================================================================
PART C -- per-s DvdK: two-sided P has CT_u[Lambda_s^m] != 0 for some small m
==============================================================================
                 P (two-sided)  r=sqrt s  first m with CT!=0
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}         1                   2
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}         2                   2
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}       1/3                   2
{(2, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(-1, 1)}         1                   3
{(2, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(-1, 1)}         2                   3
{(2, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 1): Fraction(-1, 1)}       1/3                   3
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 2): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}         1                   1
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 2): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}         2                   1
{(1, 0): Fraction(1, 1), (0, 2): Fraction(1, 1), (1, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}       1/3                   1
  DvdK guarantees such an m exists for every s (two-sided Lambda_s). CONFIRMED.

==============================================================================
PART D -- the decoupling obstruction:  L(t-1) = 0  but  t-1 != 0
==============================================================================
  L(t-1)   = 1! - 0! = 0
  L(t^2-3t+1) = 2 - 3 + 1 = 0
  So a nonzero polynomial g can have L(g) = 0.  Hence CT_u[Lambda_s^m] can be a
  NONZERO polynomial in s with L_s(...) = 0 -- integrated vanishing does NOT force
  pointwise vanishing.  THAT is the exact reason the GMC(2) descent is a real gap
  and GMC(2) is strictly harder than the (now-settled) angular TNC layer.

==============================================================================
PART E -- unconditional micro-results using the settled angular layer
==============================================================================
  E1. one-sided P (all charges > 0): E[Q P^m] = 0 for m >> deg(Q).  Example
     Q={(0, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}: E[Q P^m]=0 from m = 2 on  (deg_charge Q = 1)
     Q={(0, 3): Fraction(1, 1)}: E[Q P^m]=0 from m = 1 on  (deg_charge Q = 3)
     Q={(1, 1): Fraction(1, 1)}: E[Q P^m]=0 from m = 1 on  (deg_charge Q = 0)
  E2. two-monomial Z^p + W^q is NOT in the nullcone (charges +p, -q):
     Z^1+W^1: first m with E[P^m]!=0 is m = 2  (needs (p+q)|pm)
     Z^2+W^2: first m with E[P^m]!=0 is m = 2  (needs (p+q)|pm)
     Z^2+W^3: first m with E[P^m]!=0 is m = 5  (needs (p+q)|pm)
     Z^1+W^3: first m with E[P^m]!=0 is m = 4  (needs (p+q)|pm)

SUMMARY
  GMC(2) = (angular CT_u layer) coupled with (radial Laplace L layer).
  ANGULAR layer = TNC = Duistermaat-van der Kallen: SETTLED (1998, THM-1630).
  Charge support is s-independent, so DvdK applies uniformly in s.
  The ENTIRE remaining GMC(2) gap (THM-1540 III) is the RADIAL descent
  'pointwise-nonzero => integrated-nonzero', blocked only by ker(L) != 0.
  GMC(2) is NOT obstructed by toral geometry; it is obstructed by Laplace determinacy.
